  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 247 AM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nFor today, surface low pressure will move from south central\nCanada towards Ontario, while surface high pressure builds over\neastern Montana and works into the western Dakotas. This will \nresult in westerly to northwesterly winds that will be breezy at\ntimes, especially east of Highway 83. Otherwise, expect another\nday of above average high temperatures reaching the 50s this \nafternoon. For Tuesday, above average temperatures will continue\nas a west CONUS ridge begins to amplify creating northwesterly \nflow aloft across the state. Embedded shortwave energy within \nthis flow aloft could produce light rain from northwest to \nsoutheast. Depending on timing, a few flakes of snow could mix\nin as well in the northwest early Tuesday morning.\n\nBeyond Tuesday, aforementioned ridge continues to amplify and\nvery slowly drift eastward. As is often the case in these\nsituations, the eastern half of the state is now favored to \nremain on the cooler side of the ridge with the western half on \nthe warmer side. This translates to near to slightly below\naverage highs east with above average highs west for the latter\nhalf of the workweek. By this weekend, the ridge should be in \nthe process of breaking down as it slides fully over the \nNorthern Plains bringing above average temperatures across the \nentire state this weekend and into early next week. Mostly dry \nconditions are forecast throughout this timeframe, although \nthere is a low chance that weak shortwave energy could bring \nlight precipitation to the area late this weekend or early next \nweek.",
